clusters.verdict_basis:
O'Keefe Media Group did publish the video, released Aug. 8, 2026, under that headline, so the attribution is correct. The group's own write-up says something narrower. Poll workers confirmed that each man's name was in the poll book and that a registered voter listed there could cast a ballot (
okeefemediagroup.com
). That is not footage of a non-citizen being handed a ballot. The group also says Masheli told its reporter he planned to vote later that day (
okeefemediagroup.com
). The citizenship claims rest on OMG's reading of federal court and DHS records. I found no confirmation from Michigan election officials or an established news outlet that either man is a non-citizen or cast a ballot in the August primary. The only reports I found were from friendly outlets such as the Post Millennial and Michigan News Source. The ID point is also unproven. The video shows no one being issued a ballot without ID, and I did not verify Michigan's no-ID rules. So the claim presents a registration-roll and poll-worker allegation as established fact ("exposed… were allowed to vote… without ID"). The primary-day voting and no-ID parts are not shown.
